import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Retro — gate 3: the loop-proposing rule (a measured FAIL)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Retro — gate 3: the `loop`-proposing rule (a measured FAIL)","type":"retro","date":"2026-10-16T00:00:00.000Z"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/retros/gate3-loop-proposing-rule.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/retros/gate3-loop-proposing-rule.md"}'),a={name:`graphopt/project/retros/gate3-loop-proposing-rule.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="retro-—-gate-3-the-loop-proposing-rule-a-measured-fail" tabindex="-1">Retro — gate 3: the <code>loop</code>-proposing rule (a measured FAIL) <a class="header-anchor" href="#retro-—-gate-3-the-loop-proposing-rule-a-measured-fail" aria-label="Permalink to “Retro — gate 3: the loop-proposing rule (a measured FAIL)”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Commit</strong>: <code>b7ba9ff</code> (catopt <code>main</code>). <strong>Decides</strong>: ADR 0008 gate 3 → <strong>FAIL</strong>; ADR 0009 stage 4 is <strong>not</strong> justified on this evidence.</p><h2 id="what-the-gate-was" tabindex="-1">What the gate was <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-gate-was" aria-label="Permalink to “What the gate was”">​</a></h2><p>ADR 0008&#39;s gate 3 asked the one question a presentation cannot answer about itself: <strong>does the search reach <code>loop</code>-headed forms?</strong> Gate 2 proved the <em>spelling</em> works — a hand-written <code>loop(step, n)</code> term lowers and verifies at 1e-7 against <code>zoo._ChunkedRetention</code>. Gate 3 asked whether <em>search</em>, not the author, gets there. The 2026-10-15 run returned PARTIAL: the extracted winner reaches the algorithmic content (a chained shared-state fold, <code>reads=2 chained=True</code>, fold-chain depth 2 against the spelled 3) and projects through the bridge EQUAL — but it is never <code>loop</code>-<strong>headed</strong>, because nothing in the search can <em>introduce</em> a <code>loop</code>. The ADR&#39;s own words: <em>&quot;the fold must be proposed by search, never recovered by the bridge.&quot;</em></p><p>So the missing piece was a <strong>proposing rule</strong> — a rewrite whose right-hand side introduces <code>loop</code>.</p><h2 id="what-was-built" tabindex="-1">What was built <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-was-built" aria-label="Permalink to “What was built”">​</a></h2><p><code>lab/bridge/loop_propose.py</code> (+ <code>--propose-loop</code> in <code>lab/bridge/combinator_probe.py</code>; tests in <code>tests/test_loop_propose.py</code>).</p><p>The rule lives over the <strong>combinator presentation</strong>, which is where <code>Loop</code> actually lives (option (b) of the brief). It is</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>unroll(step, n)  →  Loop(step, n)</span></span></code></pre></div><ul><li><code>unroll(step, n)</code> is the presentation&#39;s <em>spelled</em> bounded scan: the <strong>same</strong> <code>step</code> morphism applied <code>n</code> times, with the wire bookkeeping made explicit — a <code>perm</code> before each step, one closing <code>perm</code>. It is a genuine, regular structure (one shared <code>step</code> subterm), not a hand-picked pattern.</li><li>The rule recovers <code>(step, n)</code> structurally (<code>n ≥ 2</code>; <code>unroll(step, 1)</code> is just <code>step</code>, so allowing it would make every morphism a &quot;fold&quot;) and rewrites to the combinator.</li><li><strong>Soundness</strong>: the tests prove <code>unroll(step, n)</code> and <code>Loop(step, n)</code> denote the same morphism — <code>comb_to_dag</code> expansion (i.e. <code>_apply</code>) agrees for <code>n = 1..4</code>, and the arities match (<code>S ⊗ X^n → S ⊗ Y^n</code>).</li></ul><p>The &quot;search&quot; is <code>saturate_comb</code> — a deliberately small term-level rewrite closure (apply every rule at every node to a fixpoint, dedupe, extract the cheapest by node count). No e-graph congruence: the point is reachability + selection, not speed. A real combinator search is ADR-0009 stage-4 work; this module measures whether that work has a target at all.</p><h2 id="the-commands-and-the-numbers" tabindex="-1">The commands and the numbers <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-commands-and-the-numbers" aria-label="Permalink to “The commands and the numbers”">​</a></h2><div class="language-sh"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;">#</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#BBBBBB;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> gate 3, unchanged protocol, rule armed</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">.venv/bin/python</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> lab/bridge/combinator_probe.py</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#005CC5;"> --gate2</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#005CC5;"> --extracted</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#005CC5;"> --propose-loop</span></span></code></pre></div><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>extracted term: dag=139  reads=2 chained=True</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>projects: round-trip EQUAL  morph=1590 dup=92 copies=184</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>fold-chain depth: spelled=3  extract=2</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>the presentation&#39;s spelled scan is in the search&#39;s root e-class: False</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>-- gate 3: the \`loop\`-proposing rule --</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   loop-propose: winner morph=1590 spelled-scan subterms (n&gt;=2) found=0 ns=[]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>      saturation: terms=1 fired={&#39;loop_propose&#39;: 0, &#39;loop_expand&#39;: 0}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        loop-headed reachable=False  best_is_loop=False  best_cost=1590</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   control (authored spelled scan): spelled-scan subterms=1 ns=[4]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>      fired={&#39;loop_propose&#39;: 1, &#39;loop_expand&#39;: 0}  loop-headed reachable=True</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>      best_is_loop=True  best_cost=69 (spelled 285)</span></span></code></pre></div><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>seed</th><th>spelled-scan subterms (n≥2)</th><th>rule fires</th><th>loop-headed reachable</th><th>best</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>winner</strong> (the gate)</td><td><strong>0</strong></td><td><strong>0×</strong></td><td><strong>False</strong></td><td>flat compilation, 1590</td></tr><tr><td>control (authored <code>unroll(step,4)</code>)</td><td>1 (n=4)</td><td>1×</td><td>True</td><td><code>Loop</code>, 69</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="verdict-—-fail-against-the-adr-s-criterion" tabindex="-1">Verdict — FAIL, against the ADR&#39;s criterion <a class="header-anchor" href="#verdict-—-fail-against-the-adr-s-criterion" aria-label="Permalink to “Verdict — FAIL, against the ADR&#39;s criterion”">​</a></h2><blockquote><p><strong>FAIL</strong> = the winner is the spelled/unrolled form again → search did not reach <code>loop</code>.</p></blockquote><p>The winner is the spelled/unrolled form again. The rule fires <strong>0×</strong> on it and the closure never reaches a <code>loop</code>. Two independent measurements agree:</p><ol><li><strong>The rule is well-formed.</strong> On the authored spelled scan it fires and the cheapest reachable term <em>is</em> <code>Loop</code>-headed (69 nodes against the spelled 285). This is the control — it separates &quot;the rewrite works&quot; from &quot;the search reaches it&quot;. It is not the gate.</li><li><strong>The search never reaches it.</strong> The winner&#39;s projection is a flat DAG compilation with <strong>zero</strong> <code>unroll(step, n≥2)</code> subterms. Independently, the presentation&#39;s spelled scan (the gate-2 program) lands in a <strong>different e-class</strong> from the root — the saturation simply never derives that spelling.</li></ol><h2 id="why-the-useful-part" tabindex="-1">Why (the useful part) <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-the-useful-part" aria-label="Permalink to “Why (the useful part)”">​</a></h2><p>The reason is measured and is deeper than &quot;the rule was too narrow&quot;:</p><ul><li><strong>The export&#39;s unrolled recurrence is <em>parametrically</em> regular but not <em>syntactically</em> regular.</strong> Each block&#39;s step differs by the block index (<code>split(…, index=i)</code>) and the reassociation re-spells the decay rate (<code>xi_C</code> outside, <code>xi_1</code> inside), so the K steps are K <em>distinct</em> nodes. There is no repeated <code>step</code> subterm to bind, and no fixed syntactic pattern matches the chain.</li><li><strong>The presentation&#39;s whole advantage — a <em>single shared</em> <code>step</code> — is exactly what the flat DAG erased.</strong> <code>unroll(step, n)</code> is regular <em>because</em> the author wrote it with one <code>step</code>; the search&#39;s term is regular only up to the parameter (the block index), and syntactic congruence cannot see through a parameter.</li></ul><p>So reaching the loop needs <strong>abstraction over the step</strong> — a form-level / parametric move (recognise K subterms that agree modulo the index, bind the index, and fold) — not an e-graph rewrite. The presentation makes the loop <strong>expressible</strong> (gate 2, PASS); it does not by itself make it <strong>findable</strong>.</p><h2 id="what-it-does-and-does-not-prove" tabindex="-1">What it does and does not prove <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-it-does-and-does-not-prove" aria-label="Permalink to “What it does and does not prove”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Does prove:</strong></p><ul><li>The proposing rule exists, is sound (<code>unroll ≡ Loop</code>), and <em>works</em> on the presentation&#39;s spelled scan (control PASS).</li><li>On the real gate-3 export the search does <strong>not</strong> reach a <code>loop</code>-headed form, and the presentation&#39;s spelled form is not even in the search&#39;s root e-class.</li><li>Therefore <strong>the bridge is a presentation, not a search space</strong> — the ADR&#39;s own FAIL branch, stated with numbers.</li></ul><p><strong>Does not prove:</strong></p><ul><li>That <em>no</em> proposing rule could ever work — only that a presentation-level syntactic rule cannot, and <em>why</em> (parametric, not syntactic, regularity). A form-level proposer is not ruled out; it is named as the missing piece.</li><li>Anything about whether a <code>catopt-kernel</code> package is a good idea on <em>other</em> grounds (navigability, the law/carrier split, the ports). It rules out exactly one justification — &quot;the presentation is a discovery substrate&quot; — which is the one gate 3 existed to test.</li></ul><h2 id="consequence-for-adr-0009-stage-4" tabindex="-1">Consequence for ADR 0009 stage 4 <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequence-for-adr-0009-stage-4" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequence for ADR 0009 stage 4”">​</a></h2><p>Stage 4 (&quot;promote the calculus to a dimension-named package&quot;) was <strong>gated on gate 3</strong>. Gate 3 measured negative, so on this evidence stage 4 is <strong>not</strong> justified by the presentation&#39;s discovery value. This is the outcome ADR 0009&#39;s falsification clause asked to be measured and written down rather than deferred again. If stage 4 proceeds, it must rest on a different argument (and a real form-level proposer is the piece of work that would re-open the gate).</p><h2 id="cost" tabindex="-1">Cost <a class="header-anchor" href="#cost" aria-label="Permalink to “Cost”">​</a></h2><ul><li>~40 s wall for the full <code>--gate2 --extracted --propose-loop</code> run (gate 2 verify + one 15–16 s staged saturation + the closure); peak RSS well under 1 GB — no memory pressure.</li><li>The closure is trivial on the winner (1 term, 0 fires). The control&#39;s closure is bounded by <code>max_terms=4000</code>; it is not tuned for speed because speed is not the question.</li><li>New surface: one lab module (~360 lines), one test file (10 tests), ~40 lines of probe wiring. No shipped-package change, no law-bank change, no <code>catopt_core.ir</code> node kind (the rule is over the presentation, not the IR).</li></ul><h2 id="gates-run" tabindex="-1">Gates run <a class="header-anchor" href="#gates-run" aria-label="Permalink to “Gates run”">​</a></h2><p><code>.venv/bin/ruff check lab tests/test_loop_propose.py</code>, <code>ruff format --check</code> (clean), <code>.venv/bin/ty check</code> (clean), <code>.venv/bin/vulture</code> (clean), <code>.venv/bin/lint-imports</code> (4 kept, 0 broken), <code>scripts/radon_ratchet.py</code> (ok), and <code>pytest tests/test_combinator_bridge.py tests/test_loop_propose.py</code> (22 passed). The full suite was <strong>not</strong> run (memory/time budget; no shipped-package change).</p>`,34)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};